% =============================================================================
% dill sequent rules — principal-indexed possession (TODO_0276; THY_0045/0046)
% =============================================================================
%
% Loaded as [gill.rules, dill.rules]: gill's whole graded fragment inherited by
% reference; dill adds only the `poss` modality (surface `says K A`). Two rules,
% no residual predicate — no-cross-principal-collapse is enforced by INDEX
% UNIFICATION (poss_l reuses the grade var E on hypothesis and conclusion), not
% arithmetic. The `<- !le 0 E` premise is THY_0045's fence V ("well-sorted
% principal"): it grounds E to the concrete principal of the conclusion, so the
% rule never fires with a freely-synthesized index (drop it and the refutations
% below collapse — E would unify with anything).
%
% poss_r (affirmation intro, says-I / RES_0003): K affirms any derivable A.
% poss_l (sticky elimination): `says E A` is usable only toward a `says E`-goal —
%   the SAME principal E on hypothesis and conclusion. This unification IS the
%   partial-⊕ (THY_0045 Fact P1): `says k1 A` eliminates only toward `says k1 C`,
%   never `says k2 C`. There is deliberately NO dereliction to a bare goal
%   (`says K A` ⊬ A — says is non-degenerate, Garg et al. 2006), so principals
%   never leak their content into the objective zone. Together (verified in
%   tests/engine/dill-possession.test.js):
%     derivable:  says k a            |- says k a       (identity)
%                 says k (says k b)   |- says k b       ([μ] diagonal, K1=K2)
%                 a                   |- says k (!!_0 a) (orthogonal comp., Lemma O)
%     refuted:    says k1 a           |- says k2 a       (no cross-principal collapse)
%                 says k1 (says k2 b) |- says k1 b       (nested, K1≠K2)
%                 says k a            |- a               (non-degeneracy)

@formulas A, B, C

poss_r: G ; D |- says E A
  <- G ; D |- A
  <- !le 0 E
  @pretty "saysR"
  @invertible false.

poss_l: G ; D, says E A |- says E C
  <- G ; D, A |- says E C
  <- !le 0 E
  @side l
  @pretty "saysL"
  @invertible false.
